    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Moving from Texas to Seattle is a bigger shift than the map suggests. You will trade summer heat for mild, dry summers and a long, drizzly wet season. You will keep one thing Texans love, which is no state income tax on wages, but the rest of the cost picture looks different. And the housing itself, from craftsman bungalows to modern townhomes, will feel unlike most Texas suburbs. Here is a practical guide to help you plan the move and find the right neighborhood.</p>

<h2>Taxes: What Changes When Moving From Texas to Seattle</h2>
<h3>Income Tax</h3>
<p>Good news first: neither Texas nor Washington has a broad state personal income tax on wages. Your paycheck will feel familiar in that sense. Washington does have a state capital gains excise tax on certain high-dollar long-term gains, though sales of real estate are generally exempt. If you have significant investments, a tax professional can explain how it might apply to you.</p>
<h3>Property Tax</h3>
<p>Texas is known for relatively high property tax rates. Washington's rates are often lower as a percentage of assessed value, but Seattle home values tend to be higher, so actual bills can be similar to, or even higher than, what you paid in Texas. Look up a specific property's tax history through <a href="https://kingcounty.gov/">King County</a> before you make assumptions.</p>
<h3>Sales Tax</h3>
<p>Both states rely heavily on sales tax. Seattle's combined state and local sales tax rate is among the higher rates in the country, so everyday purchases may cost a bit more than you are used to. The <a href="https://dor.wa.gov/">Washington State Department of Revenue</a> lists current rates by location.</p>
<h3>Real Estate Excise Tax</h3>
<p>One cost Texans are often unfamiliar with is Washington's real estate excise tax, which applies when a home sells. It is graduated by sale price and customarily paid by the seller, and Seattle adds a local portion. As a buyer, you will not usually pay it, but it is worth knowing about for the day you sell.</p>

<h2>Weather: Gray Winters, Glorious Summers</h2>
<p>Seattle's reputation for rain is partly earned. From roughly October through April, expect many cloudy days and frequent light rain. What surprises Texans is that it is usually more drizzle than downpour, with fewer dramatic storms than you are used to. Snow is occasional and usually brief in the city.</p>
<p>Then summer arrives. Days are long, humidity is low, and temperatures are typically comfortable. Many homes, especially older ones, were built without central air conditioning, though more people are adding heat pumps that provide both heating and cooling. If you love outdoor time, the mountains, lakes, and Puget Sound are close.</p>
<p>A few habits that help newcomers: invest in a good rain shell rather than an umbrella, get outside even on gray days, and consider a light therapy lamp for the darkest weeks of winter.</p>

<h2>Housing: Expect Different Styles and Smaller Lots</h2>
<p>Many Texas buyers are used to newer homes on generous suburban lots. Seattle's housing is more varied and often older:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Craftsman bungalows and older single-family homes</strong> in neighborhoods like Wallingford, Queen Anne, and Green Lake, often on smaller lots with mature trees.</li>
<li><strong>Townhomes</strong>, very common in Ballard, Fremont, and Greenwood, offering newer construction at a lower entry point than detached homes.</li>
<li><strong>Condos</strong> in denser areas like Capitol Hill and South Lake Union.</li>
<li><strong>Accessory dwelling units</strong>, since Seattle allows both attached ADUs and backyard cottages, which can add flexibility for guests, family, or rental income.</li>
</ul>
<p>Washington's 2023 middle-housing law also expanded where duplexes and fourplexes can be built, so you may see more variety in housing types over time. Inspections also look different here: sewer scopes, roof moss, drainage, and old heating oil tanks are common topics.</p>

<h2>Commuting and Transit</h2>
<p>Texas cities are built around freeways. Seattle has freeways too, and traffic can be heavy, but geography shapes everything. Water and hills create bottlenecks, and bridges matter. Many residents mix driving, buses, biking, and light rail.</p>
<p>Link light rail has expanded north toward Lynnwood and east toward Bellevue and Redmond, which opens up more options for car-light living. <a href="https://www.soundtransit.org/">Sound Transit</a> has current maps and service information. When choosing a neighborhood, try your actual commute at rush hour before you commit.</p>

<h2>Neighborhoods Texas Transplants Often Love</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong><a href="/neighborhoods/ballard/">Ballard</a></strong> for a lively main street, breweries, restaurants, and a strong sense of community.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/neighborhoods/west-seattle/">West Seattle</a></strong> for a slightly separate, small-town feel, beaches, and views.</li>
<li><strong>Green Lake</strong> for an active lifestyle around the lake loop and easy access to north Seattle.</li>
<li><strong>Magnolia</strong> for quieter streets, parks, and a more suburban pace inside city limits.</li>
<li><strong>Columbia City</strong> for a walkable historic core, diverse dining, and light rail access.</li>
</ul>
<p>Explore all of our <a href="/neighborhoods/">Seattle neighborhood guides</a> to compare housing styles, commutes, and lifestyle.</p>

<h2>Tips for a Smooth Move</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Visit in the wet season if you can.</strong> If you love Seattle in November, you will be happy here year-round.</li>
<li><strong>Get pre-approved early.</strong> Seattle offers often move quickly, and some homes have offer review dates.</li>
<li><strong>Budget for the differences.</strong> Factor in property taxes, sales tax, and possibly HOA dues.</li>
<li><strong>Plan for hills and parking.</strong> Moving trucks and steep streets can be tricky, so coordinate with your movers.</li>
<li><strong>Update your vehicle registration and license</strong> within the timeframe Washington requires after becoming a resident.</li>
</ol>

